    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Although a number of studies on the pressure behavior of layered systems have been undertaken during the past two decades, the state of knowledge is far from satisfactory for well test analysis. The problem appears to lie in the evaluation of the complex mathematical expressions that describe the analytical solution of such systems and in the large number of possible solutions. Numerical evaluations often are inefficient and painstaking, and have tended to restrict the scope of previous studies of layered systems.In this study, a numerical inversion scheme was used to evaluate the analytical solution of the layered system problem in Laplace space. The scheme was found to be efficient and afforded a convenient means for generating dimensionless pressure vs. the dimensionless time data for large numbers of layered system cases. Computer software was developed which can be used to generate PD vs. tD data for almost any case of layered system.Drawdown data for a number of cases involving wellbore storage, skin effect and differences in thicknesses, skin effects and radii among the layers was generated. The maximum number of layers considered was ten.Many interesting new conclusions were reached about the behavior of multi-layer systems. The most important of these being that false wellbore storage effects may appear in cases involving high permeability.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
